1
00:00:00,250 --> 00:00:03,717
You changed a file, something broke, and now nothing works.

2
00:00:03,717 --> 00:00:04,363
Watch this.

3
00:00:04,363 --> 00:00:07,419
One command, and the file is back exactly as it was.

4
00:00:07,419 --> 00:00:10,944
No backup copies, no final version two, final version three.

5
00:00:10,944 --> 00:00:15,234
That's Git, and in the next ten minutes you'll learn to use it from zero.

6
00:00:15,934 --> 00:00:19,963
Git saves every version of your work, so you can always go back.

7
00:00:19,963 --> 00:00:24,180
Programmers use it every day, but it works for any folder of files.

8
00:00:24,180 --> 00:00:25,754
Scripts, notes, websites.

9
00:00:25,754 --> 00:00:27,076
No experience needed.

10
00:00:27,076 --> 00:00:29,468
Just a terminal and about ten minutes.

11
00:00:30,168 --> 00:00:32,771
Think of Git like save points in a video game.

12
00:00:32,771 --> 00:00:35,204
Every time you reach a good spot, you save.

13
00:00:35,204 --> 00:00:37,184
Each save point is called a commit.

14
00:00:37,184 --> 00:00:41,315
It has a short code, called a hash, and a message that says what changed.

15
00:00:41,315 --> 00:00:44,200
If you mess up later, you load an older save point.

16
00:00:44,200 --> 00:00:45,558
Your work is never lost.

17
00:00:46,258 --> 00:00:47,232
Here's the plan.

18
00:00:47,232 --> 00:00:49,117
Install Git and check it works.

19
00:00:49,117 --> 00:00:50,395
Tell Git who you are.

20
00:00:50,395 --> 00:00:51,855
Start tracking a folder.

21
00:00:51,855 --> 00:00:53,923
Save versions with add and commit.

22
00:00:53,923 --> 00:00:55,262
And then the fun part.

23
00:00:55,262 --> 00:00:59,216
Go back in time, undo mistakes, and try ideas safely on a branch.

24
00:00:59,916 --> 00:01:03,594
To install Git, go to git dash s c m dot com, the official site.

25
00:01:03,594 --> 00:01:07,558
On Windows, download Git for Windows and click through the installer.

26
00:01:07,558 --> 00:01:09,225
The default options are fine.

27
00:01:09,225 --> 00:01:11,810
It also gives you a terminal called Git Bash.

28
00:01:11,810 --> 00:01:15,315
On a Mac, open Terminal and type git space dash dash version.

29
00:01:15,315 --> 00:01:18,820
If Git isn't there yet, the Mac offers to install it for you.

30
00:01:18,820 --> 00:01:20,832
On Linux, use your package manager.

31
00:01:21,532 --> 00:01:24,261
Either way, check it with git dash dash version.

32
00:01:24,261 --> 00:01:26,649
If you see a version number, you're ready.

33
00:01:26,649 --> 00:01:28,412
Mine says two point four three.

34
00:01:28,412 --> 00:01:30,572
Yours might be newer, and that's fine.

35
00:01:30,572 --> 00:01:32,847
Everything in this video works the same.

36
00:01:33,547 --> 00:01:35,065
Next, tell Git who you are.

37
00:01:35,065 --> 00:01:37,033
You only do this once per computer.

38
00:01:37,033 --> 00:01:41,081
Type git config dash dash global user dot name, and your name in quotes.

39
00:01:41,081 --> 00:01:42,992
Then the same with user dot email.

40
00:01:42,992 --> 00:01:47,827
Git writes this name and email into every commit, so you always know who changed what.

41
00:01:47,827 --> 00:01:52,606
The third line makes your main timeline be called main, which is the modern standard.

42
00:01:53,306 --> 00:01:56,093
These commands print nothing, and that's normal.

43
00:01:56,093 --> 00:01:57,777
In Git, no news is good news.

44
00:01:57,777 --> 00:01:59,693
Use your own name and email here.

45
00:01:59,693 --> 00:02:04,685
If you plan to put your projects on GitHub later, use the same email you'll use there.

46
00:02:05,386 --> 00:02:09,159
Our project is a tiny budget script in a folder called budget.

47
00:02:09,159 --> 00:02:12,142
It adds up rent and food, and prints what's left.

48
00:02:12,142 --> 00:02:14,089
It doesn't matter what's inside.

49
00:02:14,089 --> 00:02:16,402
This could be any file you care about.

50
00:02:17,102 --> 00:02:19,043
Open a terminal in that folder.

51
00:02:19,043 --> 00:02:23,114
If you type git status right now, Git says, not a git repository.

52
00:02:23,114 --> 00:02:26,308
That just means Git isn't watching this folder yet.

53
00:02:26,308 --> 00:02:29,313
Don't worry, that's what the next command fixes.

54
00:02:30,014 --> 00:02:30,897
Type git init.

55
00:02:30,897 --> 00:02:35,755
Git creates a hidden folder called dot git, where it will keep every version.

56
00:02:35,755 --> 00:02:37,080
Now git status works.

57
00:02:37,080 --> 00:02:42,001
It says we're on branch main, no commits yet, and budget dot p y is untracked.

58
00:02:42,001 --> 00:02:45,724
Untracked means Git sees the file, but isn't saving it yet.

59
00:02:46,424 --> 00:02:48,765
Here's the one idea that makes Git click.

60
00:02:48,765 --> 00:02:50,021
There are three areas.

61
00:02:50,021 --> 00:02:51,963
Your folder, where you edit files.

62
00:02:51,963 --> 00:02:56,703
The staging area, which is like a box where you put the changes for your next save.

63
00:02:56,703 --> 00:02:59,388
And the history, where the saved versions live.

64
00:02:59,388 --> 00:03:01,158
git add puts a file in the box.

65
00:03:01,158 --> 00:03:03,328
git commit seals the box and saves it.

66
00:03:04,028 --> 00:03:05,916
So, git add budget dot p y.

67
00:03:05,916 --> 00:03:07,455
Then git status again.

68
00:03:07,455 --> 00:03:12,490
Now the file is green in a real terminal, under changes to be committed.

69
00:03:12,490 --> 00:03:14,938
It's in the box, ready to be saved.

70
00:03:15,638 --> 00:03:16,267
Now commit.

71
00:03:16,267 --> 00:03:20,894
git commit, dash m, and a short message in quotes that says what this version is.

72
00:03:20,894 --> 00:03:22,265
First version of budget.

73
00:03:22,265 --> 00:03:27,120
Git answers with the branch, the hash of the new commit, and how many lines it saved.

74
00:03:27,120 --> 00:03:27,692
That's it.

75
00:03:27,692 --> 00:03:29,748
You just made your first save point.

76
00:03:30,448 --> 00:03:33,369
Write messages your future self will understand.

77
00:03:33,369 --> 00:03:36,533
Add transport costs tells you exactly what happened.

78
00:03:36,533 --> 00:03:39,271
Update, or stuff, or asdf, tells you nothing.

79
00:03:39,271 --> 00:03:40,002
A good rule.

80
00:03:40,002 --> 00:03:43,470
Finish the sentence, this commit will, and keep it short.

81
00:03:44,170 --> 00:03:45,557
Let's change something.

82
00:03:45,557 --> 00:03:48,995
I add a transport line, and subtract it from what's left.

83
00:03:48,995 --> 00:03:49,839
Save the file.

84
00:03:49,839 --> 00:03:51,708
Now, what exactly did I change?

85
00:03:51,708 --> 00:03:52,734
Git can tell you.

86
00:03:53,433 --> 00:03:57,306
git status dash dash short shows an M next to budget dot p y.

87
00:03:57,306 --> 00:03:58,385
M means modified.

88
00:03:58,385 --> 00:04:00,607
And git diff shows the exact lines.

89
00:04:00,607 --> 00:04:03,019
Lines starting with a plus were added.

90
00:04:03,019 --> 00:04:05,050
Lines with a minus were removed.

91
00:04:05,050 --> 00:04:10,573
Here, one new line for transport, and the total line was replaced with the new version.

92
00:04:11,273 --> 00:04:12,067
Happy with it?

93
00:04:12,067 --> 00:04:13,485
Same two steps as before.

94
00:04:13,485 --> 00:04:15,753
git add, then git commit with a message.

95
00:04:15,753 --> 00:04:16,887
Add transport costs.

96
00:04:16,887 --> 00:04:20,687
And I'll do it once more after adding some fun money to the budget.

97
00:04:20,687 --> 00:04:21,368
Add, commit.

98
00:04:21,368 --> 00:04:23,466
That's the whole daily rhythm of Git.

99
00:04:23,466 --> 00:04:24,487
Edit, add, commit.

100
00:04:25,187 --> 00:04:27,393
Now type git log dash dash oneline.

101
00:04:27,393 --> 00:04:29,913
Here's your whole history, newest first.

102
00:04:29,913 --> 00:04:33,254
Each line is a commit, with its hash and its message.

103
00:04:33,254 --> 00:04:35,396
These hashes are your save points.

104
00:04:35,396 --> 00:04:38,547
And notice, budget dot p y is still a single file.

105
00:04:38,547 --> 00:04:39,745
No copies anywhere.

106
00:04:40,445 --> 00:04:42,491
As a picture, it looks like this.

107
00:04:42,491 --> 00:04:46,956
Three save points in a row, and the label main points at the newest one.

108
00:04:46,956 --> 00:04:49,499
Every new commit adds a dot on the right.

109
00:04:50,199 --> 00:04:52,079
Now the scary part, made easy.

110
00:04:52,079 --> 00:04:55,213
Say I accidentally delete the food line, and save.

111
00:04:55,213 --> 00:04:57,281
I run the script, and it crashes.

112
00:04:57,281 --> 00:04:59,287
Name error, food is not defined.

113
00:04:59,287 --> 00:05:01,920
And git status shows the file is modified.

114
00:05:02,620 --> 00:05:09,206
To throw away those changes and get the last saved version back, type git restore budget dot p y.

115
00:05:09,206 --> 00:05:10,089
Run it again.

116
00:05:10,089 --> 00:05:11,990
Left this month, ten thirty.

117
00:05:11,990 --> 00:05:15,386
The file is exactly how it was at the last commit.

118
00:05:16,086 --> 00:05:16,843
One warning.

119
00:05:16,843 --> 00:05:21,134
git restore throws away your unsaved changes in that file, for good.

120
00:05:21,134 --> 00:05:23,848
Git can only bring back what was committed.

121
00:05:23,848 --> 00:05:27,572
So use it when you really want the last saved version back.

122
00:05:28,271 --> 00:05:31,232
You can also go back further, to any save point.

123
00:05:31,232 --> 00:05:34,440
Take the hash of the very first commit from git log.

124
00:05:34,440 --> 00:05:39,128
Then type git restore, dash dash source equals that hash, and the file name.

125
00:05:39,128 --> 00:05:42,829
The file now looks exactly like it did in the first version.

126
00:05:43,529 --> 00:05:49,249
Run the script, and it prints thirteen fifty, the original number, before transport and fun money.

127
00:05:49,249 --> 00:05:50,824
Your history is still safe.

128
00:05:50,824 --> 00:05:51,991
Nothing was deleted.

129
00:05:51,991 --> 00:05:58,002
When you're done looking, git restore budget dot p y brings you back to the latest version, ten thirty.

130
00:05:58,702 --> 00:06:01,168
What if the mistake was already committed?

131
00:06:01,168 --> 00:06:06,157
Here, I typed food equals forty five hundred instead of four fifty, and committed it.

132
00:06:06,157 --> 00:06:09,210
Now the budget says minus three thousand and twenty.

133
00:06:09,210 --> 00:06:09,503
Oops.

134
00:06:09,503 --> 00:06:12,791
And the bad commit is right there at the top of the log.

135
00:06:13,491 --> 00:06:18,840
The safe fix is git revert, then the hash, or just the word HEAD, which means the latest commit.

136
00:06:18,840 --> 00:06:21,904
Git creates a brand new commit that undoes the bad one.

137
00:06:21,904 --> 00:06:23,743
The budget is back to ten thirty.

138
00:06:23,743 --> 00:06:25,247
And the history shows both.

139
00:06:25,247 --> 00:06:26,640
The mistake, and the fix.

140
00:06:26,640 --> 00:06:31,654
Nothing is rewritten, which is exactly what you want when other people share your project.

141
00:06:32,354 --> 00:06:34,020
So remember the difference.

142
00:06:34,020 --> 00:06:40,006
git restore is for changes you haven't committed yet, or for peeking at an old version of a file.

143
00:06:40,006 --> 00:06:45,312
git revert is for a commit that was already saved, and it undoes it with a new commit.

144
00:06:46,012 --> 00:06:48,053
Some files should never be saved.

145
00:06:48,053 --> 00:06:51,455
Logs, temporary files, the cache folder Python creates.

146
00:06:51,455 --> 00:06:55,475
Git status shows them with two question marks, meaning untracked.

147
00:06:55,475 --> 00:07:00,980
To make Git ignore them, create a file called dot gitignore, and list them, one per line.

148
00:07:00,980 --> 00:07:02,959
A folder name ends with a slash.

149
00:07:03,659 --> 00:07:06,479
Run git status again, and the clutter is gone.

150
00:07:06,479 --> 00:07:08,993
Only the new dot gitignore file shows up.

151
00:07:08,993 --> 00:07:12,732
Add it and commit it, so the rule is saved with your project.

152
00:07:12,732 --> 00:07:18,985
This is also where you list files with passwords or secret keys, so they never end up in your history.

153
00:07:19,685 --> 00:07:20,026
Bonus.

154
00:07:20,026 --> 00:07:23,259
Want to try an idea without risking your working version?

155
00:07:23,259 --> 00:07:23,996
Use a branch.

156
00:07:23,996 --> 00:07:28,761
git switch dash c savings creates a new branch called savings and moves you onto it.

157
00:07:28,761 --> 00:07:30,633
I add a savings goal, and commit.

158
00:07:30,633 --> 00:07:31,824
Little shortcut here.

159
00:07:31,824 --> 00:07:35,624
Dash a m adds every file Git already tracks and commits, in one go.

160
00:07:35,624 --> 00:07:38,403
On this branch, the budget now says seven thirty.

161
00:07:39,103 --> 00:07:43,804
git switch main takes me back, and there the budget still says ten thirty.

162
00:07:43,804 --> 00:07:47,615
The experiment lives on its own line, and main is untouched.

163
00:07:47,615 --> 00:07:49,203
Like a parallel timeline.

164
00:07:49,903 --> 00:07:50,712
Like the idea?

165
00:07:50,712 --> 00:07:53,197
Switch to main, and type git merge savings.

166
00:07:53,197 --> 00:07:57,589
The savings commit becomes part of main, and the script prints seven thirty.

167
00:07:57,589 --> 00:07:58,398
Don't like it?

168
00:07:58,398 --> 00:08:00,826
Just switch back to main and keep working.

169
00:08:00,826 --> 00:08:02,733
The branch stays out of your way.

170
00:08:03,433 --> 00:08:05,569
Three errors you'll almost certainly see.

171
00:08:05,569 --> 00:08:07,028
Fatal, not a git repository.

172
00:08:07,028 --> 00:08:10,362
Your terminal is in the wrong folder, or you never ran git init.

173
00:08:10,362 --> 00:08:12,341
Nothing to commit, working tree clean.

174
00:08:12,341 --> 00:08:17,447
You haven't changed anything since the last commit, or you forgot to save the file in your editor.

175
00:08:17,447 --> 00:08:18,906
And author identity unknown.

176
00:08:18,906 --> 00:08:23,751
You skipped the one-time setup, so run the two git config lines from the start of this video.

177
00:08:24,451 --> 00:08:26,374
Here's that last one, for real.

178
00:08:26,374 --> 00:08:29,166
Git even tells you which two commands to run.

179
00:08:29,166 --> 00:08:31,027
Read the error message slowly.

180
00:08:31,027 --> 00:08:33,633
In Git, it almost always contains the fix.

181
00:08:34,333 --> 00:08:34,794
Pro tip.

182
00:08:34,794 --> 00:08:36,583
Commit small, and commit often.

183
00:08:36,583 --> 00:08:39,179
One change, one commit, with a clear message.

184
00:08:39,179 --> 00:08:44,141
Then, when something breaks, you go back one small step instead of losing a whole day.

185
00:08:44,141 --> 00:08:47,083
And type git status before and after every command.

186
00:08:47,083 --> 00:08:50,256
It always tells you where you are, and what to do next.

187
00:08:50,956 --> 00:08:51,657
Quick recap.

188
00:08:51,657 --> 00:08:53,116
git init once per folder.

189
00:08:53,116 --> 00:08:56,152
git add to pick what to save, git commit to save it.

190
00:08:56,152 --> 00:08:57,786
git log to see your history.

191
00:08:57,786 --> 00:09:01,464
git restore to undo unsaved changes, or to load an old version.

192
00:09:01,464 --> 00:09:03,332
And git revert to undo a commit.

193
00:09:03,332 --> 00:09:06,367
Six commands, and you'll never lose your work again.

194
00:09:07,067 --> 00:09:11,164
Next on Shortcut Lab, turn a spreadsheet into charts with Python.

195
00:09:11,164 --> 00:09:15,513
A few lines of pandas and matplotlib, and your data becomes pictures.

196
00:09:15,513 --> 00:09:17,529
Subscribe, so you don't miss it.
